SM(505,{"n":"Romans 4","a":"Fr Stephen De Young Archives/Romans 4.mp3","d":3324.4,"s":[[0.16,"Come and study Holy Scriptures with us as Father Stephen DeYoung teaches verse by verse on the podcast The Whole Counsel of God."],[8.08,"Father Stephen is a former Protestant pastor and Bible teacher in the Reformed Church, but now an Orthodox priest serving at Archangel Gabriel Orthodox Church in Lafayette, Louisiana."],[21.28,"When we get started, we're going to be picking up at the beginning of St."],[26.0,"Paul's Epistle to the Romans, chapter four."],[30.16,"As I always say at the beginning of these."],[45.56,"or listen to it again."],[49.16,"To get us sort of caught up to where we are right now."],[55.72,"I'm going to start a little earlier in my uh in my call catch up here than I normally do, in that uh rather than running through Romans 1 through 3, because we're about to talk about Abraham, we're about to talk about Adam, we're about to talk about a bunch of Old Testament folks."],[78.68,"I'm going to quickly summarize the Old Testament to sort of set the stage for what we're going to talk about."],[85.88,"Because we have to have at least a little bit of background to understand the points that St."],[92.44,"Paul is going to make."],[94.28,"But before I do that, I will I will quickly talk about what we've already seen in in Romans."],[99.64,"Remember, St. Paul is writing this epistle to the church at Rome."],[103.96,"He hasn't actuallyally physic been there yet, but because of the Emperor Claudius having expelled the Jewish community from Rome, and now that Jewish community having come back, St."],[116.96,"Paul has a concern that because there's been this separation due to the imperial edict, that there might end up being two different churches in Rome."],[127.6,"There might be sort of a Jewish Christian church and then a Gentile Christian church."],[130.96,"And he doesn't want to see that happen."],[132.4,"He doesn't want to see the church kind of splinter into these ethnic groups."],[136.48,"He wants them all to be one church."],[139.2,"And so he set out sort of making that argument"],[142.76,"And as we saw, he started out uh making a historical argument that both the Gentiles and the Jews were sort of in the same predicament."],[152.6,"And when I do my Old Testament summary, I'll touch on that a little more."],[156.36,"And now in chapter three, he's sort of summarized that and he's turned uh to talking about uh the law, the Torah."],[166.86,"The first five books of the Old Testament, and what the purpose of uh that was and is."],[176.54,"Um and a lot of that has surrounded the term, we've seen justification and righteousness, and these are all the same complex of words in Greek, even though they're translated differently in English."],[189.34,"And as we said, what's at stake there, as St."],[191.66,"Paul said, he's talking about when Christ returns to judge the living and the dead."],[196.58,"There are going to be the righteous who are on his right hand who are vindicated."],[201.06,"There are going to be the wicked who are on his left hand who are going to be condemned."],[205.32,"Everyone's going to receive what's due to them."],[207.32,"And so the question here is: who is it who's going to be on that righteous side?"],[213.72,"Because that, of course, is where we want to be."],[216.88,"And one version of that within the Jewish community at the time, and likely still now, in certain segments of rabbinic Judaism, is that, well, the Jewish people are on the righteous side."],[231.94,"And everybody else."],[233.24,"The Gentiles are on the wicked side."],[236.12,"And maybe there's a few Gentiles who have sort of come around and worship the God of Israel or have been friends to Israel who might manage to get onto the right hand side too."],[246.16,"But for the most part."],[248.78,"It's them who are there."],[250.86,"And there are going to be a whole list of ways in which they argue that."],[256.22,"And as we have seen and will continue to see, St."],[258.94,"Paul is kind of dismantling each of those one at a time."],[262.76,"And one of those, as we saw, was well, they're the ones who received the Torah."],[267.16,"They're the ones who received the word Torah."],[269.4,"Law is not a great translation of that."],[273.16,"Torah in Hebrew means more teaching."],[275.81,"So it's God's teaching."],[277.25,"That includes moral teaching."],[279.67,"And how to order society, but it includes a lot of other things too."],[281.03,"It includes God telling them who he is, for example"],[289.63,"And the Greek word gnomos, which is what gets translated as law, really includes a lot more too."],[297.63,"Gnomos includes the uh everything about a people, their laws, their customs, their culture, their their religious beliefs, all of that is sort of included in the term gnomos."],[309.55,"Unfortunately for us, it wasn't a bad translation at the time, but unfortunately for us, Saint Jerome chose to translate that as Lex, which is where we get the English word law."],[322.43,"And so it kind of got boiled down to this idea of just law."],[326.03,"It's just this legal structure"],[327.19,"It's just purely commandments."],[332.21,"Because Paul talks about the law all the time."],[335.27,"And I've always interpreted it as rules."],[339.85,"And and so that's why uh and and that's why works of law make sense."],[346.86,"The works of the Torah, that's the works commanded by the Torah."],[349.66,"Uh-huh. Um, so yeah, so that's why I try to say Torah after I read law, I'll say Torah."],[357.11,"Because we need to think about the first five books of the Bible."],[359.67,"That includes the book of Genesis, for example."],[362.69,"So this isn't all."],[364.73,"There's a lot, there's a lot of narratives, stories in there."],[367.25,"That's what he's talking about"],[370.97,"And he made the point that, first of all, just having received the Torah and five dollars will get you a cup of coffee as Starbucks."],[380.35,"If you don't keep it, if you don't believe what's in it, if you don't accept the teaching, if you don't practice it, put it into practice."],[387.89,"Then just having received it, all that does is condemn you."],[390.94,"Because God revealed all this to you and you ignored it."],[394.43,"And so he he's uh made that argument."],[397.79,"We talked a little bit last time at the end, and this will help me segue into my summary of the Old Testament about what the Torah was actually for."],[408.03,"So as the Torah itself. begins, in the book of Genesis, we read about the creation of the world"],[414.47,"And God creates Adam, He creates woman to be beside him."],[419.99,"Her name doesn't become Eve until after the fall."],[422.87,"But the plan, the commandment that God gives them."],[427.53,"So this is the first commandment in the Bible."],[430.46,"It even comes before uh the tree."],[436.14,"Um the that the commandment to them is that they be fruitful and it's usually translated in English, be fruitful and multiply, fill the earth and subdue it."],[446.08,"And the idea there is God has planted this garden, Eden, and this is a place for him to live and to dwell in alongside his human creations."],[455.88,"And so if humans had done what God wanted them to do, they would have had lots of kids, grown in number, and gone out and spread throughout the earth and spread Eden through through the whole world."],[472.35,"So that the whole world would be full of God's presence, with people."],[477.81,"As we know they did the opposite."],[481.46,"And so as we're when we get to Romans 5, uh St."],[484.5,"Paul's going to talk about how instead of spreading the presence and the knowledge of God, they spread sin and death to the whole creation."],[494.76,"Through our actions, we've done this."],[497.12,"Um that perspective, the story of Christ is then Christ coming to succeed where Adam failed and to begin that process of turning the entire world, the whole cosmos into the place where God's presence is."],[517.94,"So this is why at the end of the book of Revelation, spoilers, it says there's no temple."],[524.96,"Because God is everywhere."],[527.14,"Not just in one place."],[528.9,"Uh, he's throughout the whole world."],[530.66,"As Isaiah says, his prophecy, the knowledge of God will cover the earth the way the waters cover the sea."],[538.59,"It's just everywhere."],[540.89,"So in between, before Christ comes, we have this basic problem that the whole earth has been corrupted by sin, including people."],[551.87,"And people are continuing to sin and corrupt the world and fill the world with death and violence."],[557.87,"And so this reaches a climax in Genesis 6, and there's the flood."],[562.96,"Where it all gets washed clean."],[565.34,"Start over. It happens again, Tower of Babel."],[568.71,"Because after the flood, God gives the same commandment to Noah: be fruitful, multiply, fill the earth, and subdue it."],[573.99,"And what do they do?"],[575.43,"No, let's all stay here and build a big city."],[579.41,"And the Tower of Babel' as ziggurat."],[580.85,"Let's build a big biggurat temple so we can pull God down from heaven and get him to do what we want him to do."],[587.39,"Instead of doing what he has commanded us to do."],[590.97,"And so God kind of scatters and disperses them by force out into the world."],[598.89,"And as we taught, we've talked about a couple of times in Deuteronomy 32, it talks more about this that all the nations end up getting assigned to these sort of angelic beings instead of to God himself."],[610.57,"He just sort of throws them all out there."],[612.81,"Most of those angelic beings end up falling and becoming wicked."],[617.21,"Uh, these are the powers and principalities that St."],[619.61,"Paul talks about."],[620.49,"When we get to him talking about that, we'll talk more about that."],[625.65,"But there's still this problem."],[629.01,"Yes, these are the pagan gods."],[631.97,"That's why you get all the gods of the nations are demons."],[638.69,"So we still have this problem."],[640.85,"God then creates a people for himself."],[645.33,"He grabs Abraham out of Ur, who's just in one of those nations."],[650.39,"Grabs him, pulls him out, makes these promises to him."],[655.35,"We have the Exodus."],[656.56,"We have the creation of the people of Israel."],[658.4,"And so now God wants to dwell with his newly created people of Israel."],[665.12,"Well, what's our basic problem here?"],[668.32,"They're just as sinful and wicked as everybody else."],[671.5,"And this is what St."],[672.38,"Paul's been arguing here in Romans."],[674.24,"They're just as sinful and wicked as everybody else, just as corrupt."],[677.2,"So how can God come and live among them."],[681.06,"Without them being destroyed by his holiness coming into their presence."],[688.02,"Well, this is what the Torah is about."],[691.7,"This is what the law is about."],[693.86,"It's this system for managing sin."],[698.6,"And managing corruption so that God can come and dwell among his people."],[704.2,"So you've got the tabernacle first, and then later the temple, sort of in the midst of his people, which is this place that's kept holy and set apart for God to dwell in."],[715.6,"Every year they have to have the Day of Atonement, where they come and they offer sacrifices."],[720.64,"There's the two goats at the Day of Atonement."],[722.8,"The one goat takes the sins of the people outside the camp, takes the sin away."],[727.12,"The second one is sacrificed, and the blood is used to purify and cleanse everything from the stain that's left by the people's sins that year."],[734.57,"But this has to keep happening year in and year out and year in it, because it's just managing the problem."],[740.01,"So that God doesn't either have to leave or wipe everybody out."],[744.75,"Which are the sort of the two options."],[747.15,"So it's just this management system."],[749.47,"It was never there to make people righteous."],[753.92,"If you keep the whole Torah perfectly, and St."],[756.08,"Make no mistake"],[757.4,"Paul later is going to say that he did."],[760.68,"He's going to say he was blameless in terms of the Torah."],[765.16,"Back when he was a Pharisee."],[767.8,"He's going to say, I kept all the rules."],[769.66,"Kept them all perfectly."],[771.9,"There's no credit to that."],[774.48,"You've done like the minimum that's required by God."],[781.84,"To not be a wicked person."],[783.36,"So it was never about making people righteous."],[785.6,"It's about managing this until the time when Christ came."],[790.56,"Paul's epistles, including Hebrews"],[791.16,"And we're going to see this theme over and over again all through St."],[797.88,"This is the major theme of Hebrews."],[800.45,"Is how we don't need those sacrifices from the old covenant anymore because they were just management and they had to happen every year."],[808.77,"And the high priest had to first go and act offer a sacrifice for his own sins so that he could even walk into the temple to offer the sacrifice for everybody else's sins."],[819.73,"And so now Christ has come and offered himself as a perfect sacrifice."],[824.61,"And so we don't need the management system anymore because the problem's been solved in Christ."],[831.65,"And so this is this is the understanding that St."],[835.25,"Paul is arguing for here."],[837.43,"And so he's gone through a chapter one, he talked about how historically the Gentiles following the Tower of Babel all went astray."],[844.07,"y. The had the knowledge of God"],[846.65,"They're all descended from Noah."],[847.87,"So they had at one point in the distance path, they had the knowledge of God, but they turned away and went a different direction."],[854.35,"And he talked about how, even though they had that knowledge, they suppressed it."],[859.07,"Instead of offering praise and glory to God, they offered praise and glory to other things."],[863.81,"To other things that were not God's to themselves."],[867.38,"And became thoroughly corrupt."],[869.54,"Then he turns to the Jewish people."],[872.74,"He says, God gave you this."],[874.52,"Is this and look what you did."],[877.5,"You didn't even follow that."],[879.26,"Because what happens ultimately in the Old Testament?"],[882.7,"Ultimately, in the Old Testament, the people haven't kept it."],[885.54,"They haven't followed it."],[886.38,"And so the whole land has become corrupt"],[886.46,"And so God leaves the temple."],[891.72,"It's in Ezekiel."],[892.68,"He gets up and leaves."],[893.48,"And as soon as he gets up and leaves, that's it."],[897.88,"Babylon comes in and wipes it out and takes them into exile."],[903.8,"Okay, so this is this is St."],[906.68,"Paul's understanding, and this is what leads us up to where we are now."],[911.82,"So he said to them, you know, all functionally the Torah does for you when you keep bringing it up."],[917.99,"It never was intended to, or would have, or could have made you righteous people, made you able to stand at the right hand of God at the judgment."],[927.99,"In fact, quite the opposite."],[930.01,"The fact that you received this knowledge now makes you more subject to judgment because you haven't."],[936.89,"You haven't followed it."],[938.87,"He even says, River at one point that there are Gentiles out there who have done a better job of following it without having it."],[946.45,"Than you have with all of this knowledge."],[951.01,"Okay. And so uh this culminated at the end of chapter three uh with him uh talking about, he sort of narrowed his focus to circumcision, which is one part, of course, of the Torah."],[969.51,"It's one part of the teaching."],[971.51,"And this is especially important because this is, in the view of the Jewish people at this time, this is sort of the physical emblem of the fact that they're separated from the rest of the nations."],[985.0,"That they have this special status."],[987.32,"And they're relying on this special status when the day comes when God is gonna judge the earth."],[995.0,"Well, we've got this special status, so we're gonna be fine."],[998.46,"We're the ones who he loves, and he hates all those other people."],[1002.0,"Because you know, we're the circumcised, we're the right."],[1006.68,"And remember at the at the very end, uh he points out look, God is the God of the whole cosmos."],[1015.74,"They can't argue with that, which means he's not just the God of the Jews, he's also the God of the Gentiles."],[1016.7,"He's their God, he's everyone's God"],[1024.35,"And that's why he's going to judge everyone."],[1027.31,"He says, So since there's only one God, who will justify the circumcised by faith and the uncircumcised through faith."],[1036.6,"So he says, uh, what he's already argued, remember, faith is the same word as the verb believe."],[1046.2,"And we've talked about already how belief doesn't just mean the truefalse test."],[1052.22,"Yeah, okay, I agree."],[1054.86,"And we're gonna get an example here in chapter four."],[1057.34,"But he's saying, look, the people who are gonna stand on the right hand of God are going to be the people who have been faithful, who have been loyal, who have been true to the knowledge that God has given them."],[1069.06,"And so the uncircumcised people, the Gentiles out there who have been faithful and true to what God has given them, they're going to be the ones on the right."],[1077.38,"And the people who are circumcised, the Jewish people who have been faithful and loyal to God and done what is right, are going to be the ones who are standing on God's right hand."],[1086.9,"So the dividing line isn't circumcision or any other part of the Torah, whether you eat gator or not."],[1096.03,"Whether you, you know, um, whether you showed up at the right New Moon Festival or not."],[1101.89,"That's not what does it."],[1103.81,"That's not what decides"],[1106.03,"It's this faithfulness and loyalty, which can be expressed through keeping the Torah."],[1114.52,"But it's not the same thing as keeping the Torah."],[1119.29,"Well, let me give you a more modern example of that for us."],[1122.25,"Okay. We express our faith and our belief and our trust and our faithfulness to God by coming to church on Sunday."],[1131.37,"But if you're gonna stand before the judgment seat of Christ and say, Well, yeah, but I went to church every Sunday."],[1139.98,"That's not gonna cut it."],[1142.54,"That's not gonna cut it."],[1143.88,"So it's the same thing, it's the same thing with the Torah."],[1147.26,"The Jewish people who received the Torah, for them, part of their faithfulness to God was keeping it."],[1155.11,"Was keeping it."],[1156.31,"But just having received it."],[1158.41,"Or just going through the motion, having the tassels on your garment."],[1161.31,"Doing those things."],[1162.67,"Standing in front of Christ on the day of judgment, saying, Well, yeah, but I never ate shellfish."],[1168.81,"That's not that's not gonna do it."],[1171.45,"I'm so glad that's not it."],[1173.45,"We'd be in real trouble down here."],[1175.69,"Yeah. Okay. So unless there are any uh questions or comments or rumors or innuendos or anything, we'll go ahead and get started in uh St."],[1188.17,"Paul's Epistle to the Romans. chapter 4 verse 1"],[1193.49,"What then shall we say that Abraham our father has found according to the I'm sorry, what then shall we say that Abraham our father has found according to the flesh?"],[1210.21,"That's kind of a weird way of ordering that sentence here the new king James."],[1216.45,"Uh it should be what then shall we say that Abraham, our father, according to the flesh, has found?"],[1224.21,"The idea is that right, St."],[1227.41,"Paul's saying we, our."],[1229.35,"He's still talking to Jewish people."],[1231.43,"So according to the flesh, Abraham is is their their father."],[1236.75,"He's their flesh."],[1237.63,"Linear descent. And he's gonna and that that language is important because as we've already seen in in uh St."],[1247.28,"John's Gospel, for example."],[1249.38,"Christ made a point about who was really the child of Abraham."],[1253.54,"Is it the one who is like Abraham and does what Abraham does, or is it the one who just happens to be descended from him?"],[1261.32,"St. Paul's gonna make a similar point coming up here in Romans."],[1265.72,"So he's at least, minimally, if we're talking to if he's talking to Jewish people, Abraham is their father according to the flesh."],[1274.2,"So uh what uh what is he? found"],[1278.08,"For if Abraham was justified by works, he has something to boast about, but not before God."],[1285.84,"Okay, so he's saying if Abraham ended up being on that right side, if Abraham in our modern language, if Abraham became a saint, by just what he did."],[1299.84,"He earned it."],[1301.49,"Then he'd have something to brag about."],[1304.07,"Logically. If I go and accomplish something, then I at least theoretically can brag about it."],[1310.82,"I did the hard work and I accomplished this."],[1317.94,"For what does the scri?pture say"],[1319.66,"Abraham believed God and it was accounted to him for righteousness."],[1325.26,"Now to him who works, the wages are not counted as grace, but as debt."],[1343.98,"That's not a gift."],[1347.18,"The check isn't a gift."],[1348.46,"I don't say, oh, thank you for giving me this money."],[1351.72,"Because I've earned it."],[1353.89,"Because I've earned it."],[1356.05,"And so he says, W,ell that's not what the scriptures say."],[1360.29,"Now remember what we've said about Old Testament quotes."],[1364.31,"They didn't have chapters and verses for another 1440 years yet."],[1371.83,"After after St."],[1373.19,"Paul is writing this."],[1374.87,"So when they quote one verse, they want us to understand the whole section."],[1382.41,"It's not just, oh, well, here's look hey, hey, see what this verse I found?"],[1386.25,"Proof text. Yeah, proof texting."],[1388.31,"Okay. And where this comes."],[1391.93,"This statement, Abraham has believed something in particular."],[1398.13,"God has come to Abraham and made these promises to him."],[1401.89,"He's come to Abraham and said, I'm gonna, you're gonna be the father of whole nations, plural."],[1404.13,"'re gonna be the father of. You whole nations"],[1409.34,"I'm gonna make you great people, and I'm gonna give to your seed this land."],[1415.84,"Over on the other side of the world from where you live now."],[1418.72,"Because he lived in Ur, which is in what's now Iraq."],[1422.5,"The land he was giving him is Palestine."],[1425.0,"So he says, I'm giving this to you."],[1429.96,"Now, obviously, Abram at the time, he wasn't even Abraham, named Abraham yet, didn't earn that."],[1441.2,"God just comes to him and makes this promise."],[1444.26,"It wasn't that, you know, for 40 years of faithful service, I'm going to give you this land."],[1450.78,"God just comes and says, I'm doing this."],[1452.88,"So that's grace."],[1453.68,"We talked about that's what grace is."],[1455.04,"It's God acting."],[1456.78,"As we said when I was summarizing the Old Testament, God decides I'm going to make this people for myself."],[1463.26,"And he goes and he chooses Abraham to do it."],[1468.27,"But then we're told Abraham's response."],[1473.24,"Which is that he believes God."],[1477.33,"So, first of all, he believes that that promise is true."],[1481.41,"Even though he's already an old man."],[1484.51,"He's in his 70s when he leaves Ur."],[1487.55,"By the time he has a kid, he's a hundred"],[1492.47,"Okay? But God comes to a guy in his 70s and says, Whole nations are going to be descended from you."],[1503.19,"But he believes God."],[1504.63,"Now, one interpretation that our Protestant friends use of this passage."],[1511.61,"They say, Oh, see, look, if you work, then it's wages."],[1515.13,"It's not a gift."],[1517.19,"So St. Paul's saying Abraham didn't do anything."],[1521.83,"Now he said he didn't do anything to earn the promise."],[1524.71,"But did he really did not do anything."],[1528.79,"No, he got up and got his whole family and traveled hundreds of miles across the whole Fertile Crescent to get over there to that land that God had promised him because he believed."],[1541.87,"So you can't separate his belief from the actions he took."],[1546.75,"If he had just sat there in Ur and said, Oh yeah, God, I believe you."],[1553.71,"And then went back to work the next day at whatever he was doing in Ur."],[1560.15,"That would be the same thing."],[1561.43,"So belief required him to do something."],[1566.49,"So it's not just belief in the sense of checking about, oh yeah, I believe you."],[1571.82,"It includes faithfulness."],[1573.26,"Paul isn't saying Abraham didn't do anything"],[1574.58,"So he went and did something."],[1576.47,"So St. And God just said, Oh, you're righteous because I told you this, and you said okay."],[1588.49,"That's not what it's saying."],[1590.51,"It's saying because Abraham responded to God's promise in a certain way."],[1597.77,"I suppose theoretically."],[1598.97,"Which included belief in action."],[1603.29,"God considered him to be a righteous man."],[1608.67,"He's on the right side."],[1610.43,"This is how he becomes a saint."],[1612.35,"This is how he becomes one of the holy ones."],[1614.52,"By his response. to God's promise"],[1617.52,"Well, with the Protestant interpretation, theoretically, he could have believed it and sat there and said, okay, I can't wait to watch this happen."],[1628.62,"Because he would think that God would make it come true."],[1633.5,"Would just make it happen."],[1634.54,"Just make it happen."],[1635.82,"Yeah. Yeah. So he had to, he, he, he, he did something."],[1642.48,"He didn't do anything to earn the promise."],[1646.72,"But he received the promise."],[1648.8,"He was a righteous man because of what he did based on his belief."],[1656.4,"Based on his faith."],[1658.08,"And there are plenty of other examples of the opposite in the Old Testament"],[1665.1,"One of the really obvious ones."],[1667.15,"God comes to David when David is made king."],[1671.95,"And he says, If you follow all of my ways and keep all of my commandments, you keep the Torah, you follow the Torah, I will make your descendants, I'll make a descendant of yours rule on the throne forever."],[1690.27,"And so we're told that David did, which tells us something about repentance being included to keep the Torah, because he committed plenty of sins."],[1700.6,"But he repented of those sins."],[1702.28,"And so. the Messiah, is ultimately born from his line"],[1706.5,"But what fewer people remember is that after his son Solomon was judged, and the kingdom of Israel gets split into two parts Judah in the south and Israel in the north."],[1717.8,"And so one of David's descendants continues to reign over Judah in the south, which is much smaller."],[1723.24,"The bigger kingdom in the north gets given first to Jeroboam the son of Nebat."],[1728.76,"And when Jeroboam the son of Nebat becomes king of the northern kingdom of Israel, God comes to him and says, If you follow all my ways, keep all my commandments, a descendant of yours will sit on the throne of this kingdom in Israel forever."],[1746.2,"It's the same prom.ise he made to David"],[1750.88,"But Jeroboam doesn't do that."],[1752.66,"He sets up idols, he breaks off everything, you know, disregards God."],[1758.32,"And so his son ends up getting assassinated."],[1761.93,"And the new king who assassinated him comes, and guess what?"],[1766.57,"God goes to him and makes the same promise."],[1772.7,"So at least hypothetically, now of course, God knows everything in his providence, but for the sake of a hypothetical thought experiment."],[1782.43,"What if Abraham had said, You're crazy?"],[1787.23,"I've got things good here in Ur."],[1789.07,"I ain't having any kids"],[1789.43,"I'm pretty well off."],[1790.71,"I'm 75 years old."],[1799.12,"Well, going by what happened with Jeroboam, God would have gone to someone else and made the same promise."],[1806.88,"We've got to remember this response."],[1809.36,"And this is this is, we talk about this dynamic again a lot around the annunciation."],[1816.33,"Because God comes to the Theotokos."],[1819.53,"He comes to her and he says, You're going to have a child."],[1824.26,"And what's her response?"],[1826.82,"Let it be to me according to your will."],[1829.62,"She says, Yes."],[1832.1,"So St She accepts it."],[1835.58,"Paul is not arguing that God does everything here."],[1843.18,"But he's arguing that God comes to us first."],[1847.68,"As we've talked about before, God didn't wait for the people of Israel in Egypt in slavery."],[1854.56,"Say, okay, when you guys, I'll give you the Torah now, and when you guys get your act together and you all follow this perfectly, then I'll deliver you from Egypt and take you to the land."],[1864.16,"He doesn't He takes them out of Egypt."],[1870.09,"He takes it to the light."],[1871.29,"But then he gives them the Torah."],[1874.59,"And he says, follow this."],[1876.39,"And their initial response is, oh yes."],[1880.17,"At Mount Sinai."],[1881.69,"It's oh sure."],[1882.73,"Yeah, we're gonna do all of this."],[1885.21,"Then they don't."],[1887.13,"But there's always these two."],[1888.49,"And St. Paul isn't arguing against that."],[1890.25,"He's talking about that God has moved first."],[1893.61,"And of course, the the paradigm of that is that, and he's gonna say this explicitly in a little bit, is that Christ came before everyone in this room was born."],[1908.45,"And died to save us from our sins, died to give us eternal life before we existed."],[1915.65,"Okay, so we didn't move first."],[1918.59,"God moves first."],[1919.79,"But then we respond."],[1921.47,"We respond in faith, or we don't."],[1924.75,"And faith isn't just, yeah, sounds good."],[1928.21,"Faith involves then doing something."],[1932.37,"If you really believe, in our case."],[1935.11,"In our case, for Abraham, the promise was about his descendants in this land."],[1939.59,"For us, the promise is that Christ is returning to judge the living and the dead."],[1943.27,"And that we're going to have to give an account for all of our actions."],[1953.11,"But if we really believe it, that's going to change how we live our life."],[1956.95,"That's going to change how we look at our at the sin in our life and repentance."],[1961.59,"If we really believe that's going to happen."],[1965.55,"It's the same kind of thing."],[1966.83,"Faith and response can't be separated from each other."],[1974.11,"And St. Paul isn't doing that here."],[1978.35,"But he's making the point."],[1979.79,"Remember, one of the big arguments of the Pharisees, we talked about this when we were going through the Gospels, was it was apparent to everyone in the Jewish people that even though they had rebuilt the temple, and even though a good chunk of them had come back from exile and were now living in Judea."],[1997.35,"They were still under the Romans."],[1999.92,"God's presence hadn't returned to the temple the way he had been present in the temple before."],[2005.76,"So something was still wrong"],[2007.61,"How did they know that?"],[2009.69,"Well, because they read in the scriptures what happened when the tabernacle was dedicated."],[2014.57,"And the presence of God filled it, and there was fire and light and a cloud, and you know, same thing happened when the temple was dedicated."],[2024.65,"And Ezekiel then has this vision of God's presence leaving the temple before it was destroyed."],[2029.93,"Well, they rebuilt the temple."],[2032.25,"That didn't happen."],[2033.45,"And it didn't happen."],[2035.93,"And so in their minds, they're still in exile."],[2040.33,"And they have to figure out why."],[2042.73,"And the primary answer the Pharisees gave for why was, well, look, we got sent into exile because we didn't keep the Torah."],[2052.79,"So the way to end the exile is for all of us to keep the Torah."],[2058.47,"And once we all get it right, and we're all living these pure and holy lives, then God will send the Messiah to kick the Romans out and redeem us, and he'll return to his temple, and everything will be okay again."],[2071.91,"Jewish rabbi told me that they believe that if all the Jews kept the Torah for one day, the Messiah would come."],[2082.09,"And that's exactly it."],[2084.01,"That's where it comes from."],[2085.37,"So this is their idea."],[2086.49,"And this is why, as we see in the Gospels, the Pharisees so hated tax collectors and sinners and these people."],[2094.33,"It wasn't just that they were sort of holier than thou."],[2097.57,"It's that these people are the ones who are keeping us under the Roman boot."],[2102.69,"They're keeping God from returning to our temple."],[2105.79,"They're destroying our whole community."],[2107.95,"And so they want to be rid of them, including killing them."],[2110.97,"And this is what motivated Saint Paul back when he was Saul and he was persecuting the church."],[2118.42,"Because he said, these crazy heretics think the Messiah has already come."],[2126.68,"They're leading people astray."],[2130.28,"So I need to go and put an end to them."],[2133.98,"And then, of course, he got massively corrected by Christ himself and becomes St."],[2138.38,"But so this, when he's arguing against, talking about works and earning things, this is what he's talking about"],[2138.66,"Paul. He's talking about this idea."],[2148.52,"He's saying, okay, you think that the way you're going to make now as a Jewish Christian, the way you're going to make Christ return to judge the living and the dead."],[2159.02,"And you're going to be one of the righteous."],[2160.7,"The way you're going to do that is by earning it."],[2164.55,"By following all of this perfectly."],[2168.51,"And so he said, is that how Abraham got into that category?"],[2177.67,"Was he just leading this perfect life according to Torah that didn'tn't had been given yet in Ur?"],[2185.87,"Or did God just come to him?"],[2188.03,"And he responded with faithfulness and loyalty and trust."],[2192.91,"And he acted upon that."],[2195.63,"And so St."],[2196.35,"Paul is saying it's the same thing in the church."],[2201.79,"The gospel has come to you."],[2203.31,"Whether you're a Jew or you're a Gentile, you've heard the gospel of Jesus Christ preached to you."],[2211.15,"If you respond with faithfulness, with trust, with belief."],[2217.51,"If you act, if you change your life in accordance with the fact that you believe in that, then you are one of you are one of the righteous."],[2225.95,"You don't have to do this other stuff besides, because that's not what this other stuff was ever for, anyway."],[2233.61,"That was for something else."],[2238.49,"So it continues, but to him who does not work, but believes on him who justifies the ungodly, his faith is accounted for righteousness."],[2250.57,"Just as David also describes the blessedness of the man to whom God imputes righteousness apart from works."],[2258.73,"Blessed are those whose lawless deeds are forgiven and whose sins are covered."],[2264.09,"Blessed is the man to whom the Lord shall not impute sin."],[2268.33,"Okay. Even after all your explanation, I don't follow and understand that paragraph of that I just read?"],[2277.49,"Well, up to up to the quotation."],[2280.61,"That's what I'm about to."],[2283.01,"Part of our problem here is even in the Orthodox Study Bible, they're using the New King James Version, which was translated by our Protestant friends."],[2293.41,"This is not, I'm not, there's nothing nefarious here."],[2297.19,"They aren't deliberately trying to like skew."],[2299.51,"Oh, we're gonna mess with this so it supports our views."],[2302.81,"It's not like that."],[2304.01,"These are the views they have, so when they read the text, this is how they read it."],[2308.91,"Naturally. Okay. So I'm gonna go, I'm gonna go through it now."],[2314.35,"But I'm gonna start."],[2315.19,"The reason I read as far as I did is again, I want to start with the scripture quote."],[2321.79,"Because. Blessed are those whose lawless deeds are forgiven and whose sins are covered."],[2327.57,"Blessed is the man to whom the Lord shall not impute or reckon sin."],[2333.73,"Okay. So our Protestant friends who have read that first paragraph want to say, okay, see, look, St."],[2340.29,"Paul is quoting David, he's talking about God just forgives people's sins."],[2345.55,"No work, no action required."],[2348.75,"This is Psalm 32 or 31 in the Greek numbering."],[2353.71,"Okay. And these are the first two verses that St."],[2356.83,"Paul quotes. Blessed are those whose transgressions are forgiven and whose sins are covered."],[2362.47,"Blessed is the man whose sin the Lord does not take into account, and in whose mouth there is no deceit."],[2374.79,"Because I kept silent my bones grew old from my groaning all the day long."],[2381.43,"For day and night your hand was heavy upon me."],[2384.31,"I the said, I will confess my transgression to Lord, and you forgave the ungodliness of my sin."],[2398.07,"So did this person do nothing?"],[2401.51,"No. David's talking about himself"],[2404.61,"This person didn't do nothing."],[2408.83,"He repented of his sins."],[2410.27,"He confessed them."],[2411.15,"He said, when I was silent, I felt God's wrath."],[2416.47,"It was horrible."],[2417.43,"It was like my bones were breaking."],[2418.97,"It's his image."],[2420.49,"When I confessed my sin."],[2423.31,"When I confessed my sin, then how good it felt."],[2426.03,"And how blessed I was that my sins were forgiven and they were covered."],[2430.99,"Because I confessed them, because I repented of them."],[2435.79,"Okay? So if St."],[2438.99,"Paul were trying to argue that people's sins are forgiven without them doing anything, he picked a lousy passage to quote."],[2446.35,"And St Because that's not what the passage said."],[2452.13,"Paul was a highly trained rabbi, so he knew what the Psalms said."],[2456.85,"He's not trying to, and his readers knew what the Psalms said."],[2460.85,"He's not trying to put one over on them."],[2464.05,"Okay, so with that taking into account, okay, the work that it's talking about here, is again before the fact."],[2477.28,"We don't earn forgiveness of sins."],[2481.52,"God doesn't look at us and say, Well, your record so far has been so great that I'll just overlook this one."],[2488.9,"Yeah, everybody messes up once in a while, but you're doing such a great job."],[2492.82,"We'll just let this one slide."],[2496.32,"The fact that God comes and offers us forgiveness of sins if we repent is grace."],[2504.56,"He doesn't have to do that."],[2508.13,"He doesn't have to forgive us."],[2510.29,"We could say, I'm so sorry, and he could say, too bad."],[2513.65,"What you did was terrible."],[2517.01,"I mean, he would be within his rights."],[2519.75,"You knew better."],[2521.84,"You did it deliberately."],[2524.0,"So the fact that God has offered us forgiveness of sins."],[2529.76,"Remember, this is is how the gospel described in Acts that repentance and forgiveness of sins were preached in the name of Jesus Christ."],[2530.32,"how the gospel is described. That's"],[2537.96,"So that God has made this path for us, that Christ has died for our sins, has given us this way to have our sins covered."],[2547.0,"God did that before we were born."],[2550.28,"We didn't earn that."],[2553.0,"Okay. But is that the same thing as saying it doesn't require us to do anything?"],[2561.45,"God comes to us again with a promise."],[2564.97,"To quote 1 John, if we confess our sins, he is faithful and just to forgive us our sins and cleanse us from all unrighteousness."],[2571.85,"He makes us that promise."],[2574.17,"But for us to receive that promise, number one, we have to believe that it's true, that he will forgive them if we confess."],[2581.09,"And then we have to actually confess them."],[2583.17,"We have to actually repent."],[2585.57,"We have to actually change our life and try and do differently and do better."],[2591.65,"So the promise is something we haven't earned."],[2595.97,"God doesn't owe us forgiveness."],[2599.73,"There's nothing mechanical about it."],[2601.73,"Where if we repent, God He has to."],[2606.21,"That's just the way things work."],[2607.81,"But He's made us this promise."],[2611.47,"so if we do it, he is faithful, And and he is just."],[2615.35,"And so God always keeps His promises once He's made them."],[2619.27,"I've forgotten for sure, but I think I've made no, who said, God will forgive me, it's his job"],[2627.89,"Which is not the case."],[2631.81,"Which is not the case."],[2633.57,"This was not held up to me as a great example to follow, but it was I think it was King Charles II, but I'm not sure."],[2642.13,"And remember, the same thing is true of forgiveness."],[2647.09,"Forgiveness is not just God saying, o,kay yeah, okay, I won't, I won't smack you for that one later."],[2654.98,"Forgiveness is God actually healing, actually cleansing, actually purifying us."],[2662.5,"Well, yeah And isn't it convinced?"],[2664.3,"So he does something."],[2668.7,"That's yeah. That's part of that repentance aspect."],[2676.23,"We just read about Zacchaeus on Sunday."],[2680.29,"When he repented, it wasn't just like, oh yeah, I'm sorry about all that stealing and extortion I did."],[2687.17,"And Jesus said, ah, it's alright."],[2690.19,"That's not what happens in the gospel."],[2692.83,"What happens is Zacchaeus stands up and says, I'm gonna give half of what I have to the poor, and I'm gonna give back everyone I stole money from."],[2701.55,"I'm gonna give them four times what I stole from them."],[2704.11,"And after he says that, Jesus says, Today salvation has come to this house."],[2706.27,"'s. Because that what repentance is"],[2712.53,"Right? That's what repentance is."],[2715.09,"And we have the parable of the unmerciful servant, remember, where he gets forgiven this huge debt, equivalent of millions of dollars, and then he's walking out and he runs into a guy who owes him five bucks and he grabs him and starts showing you and says, Where's my money?"],[2731.67,"And the point is not that, oh hey, God will let you slide on this stuff as long as you meet these requirements."],[2739.36,"It's that showed he hadn't repented."],[2742.96,"He had no idea the depths of the forgiveness he had just received."],[2747.44,"Because if he had, if he did understand that, there's no way he'd go and rough a guy up for five bucks after he just got forgiven a multi-million dollar debt"],[2757.3,"He would be going around forgiving everybody."],[2760.24,"Tearing up all his creditors, you know, if he really understood."],[2767.36,"So this is this is what's important."],[2769.52,"Because remember, look, St."],[2771.2,"Paul says, just as David also describes."],[2779.0,"So if you're going to argue that what he's saying is different than what David's saying, you're going to have a problem."],[2785.93,"So the point St."],[2786.97,"Paul is making, once again, God doesn't owe us forgiveness either, but he's promised it to us."],[2793.13,"We haven't earned it."],[2794.75,"But in order to receive that promise, it requires from us belief, faith, and that expresses itself in actions."],[2805.36,"Expresses itself in actual actions."],[2807.44,"In this case, with forgiveness of sins, repentance."],[2811.12,"In Abraham's case, to receive the promise, picking up your family and traveling hundreds of miles."],[2817.52,"So at the time of St."],[2819.04,"Paul, what are the actions based on?"],[2823.68,"You know, like, did they follow exactly what Jesus asked them to do, even though it was harder than the Old Testament uh instructions?"],[2831.84,"Or you know, what were those actions at that time?"],[2834.88,"Well, in a lot of cases, well, for example, Zacchaeus, um, the giving back four times what he stole actually comes from the Torah."],[2843.48,"That's what you were supposed to do if you were a thief."],[2846.38,"So he was doing it voluntarily."],[2848.22,"But the part about giving half of his possessions to the poor was beyond was beyond what was required in the Torah in the Old Testament."],[2860.83,"So there's not there's not a list of actions like, okay, I've done this and I've done this and I've done this, so I'm forgiven."],[2869.57,"It's there's a whole, there's a whole, it has to it's a whole transformation of your life."],[2876.89,"And when we get toward the end of Romans, St."],[2879.45,"Um, all of his epistles are in two parts"],[2879.81,"Paul's gonna start laying that out at the end."],[2885.01,"There's a part where he talks about theology, and then he says, Therefore, because all these things are true, therefore, this is what you need to do."],[2892.71,"And he lays out what you need to do."],[2895.35,"But even those aren't just like a list of things."],[2898.6,"Like you need to have tassels on your robe, and you need to not eat this and eat that instead."],[2903.78,"It's you need to love your neighbor, you need to give, you know, freely of what you have."],[2909.46,"You need to um you know, you need to repent when you have sinned and try to make things right with your brother."],[2916.36,"All of these things."],[2917.16,"You have to practice self-control."],[2919.66,"You need to all of all of these things."],[2922.24,"That aren't just things you can check off."],[2924.16,"Like, oh yeah, I practice self-control today, check."],[2928.8,"Or I I was loving today, check."],[2931.57,"They're all encompassing."],[2933.25,"Would this add up to the teachings of Christ in the in the Sermon on the Mount?"],[2941.09,"Sermon on the Mount."],[2942.45,"Yeah, I mean, this is going to stay in parallel."],[2944.83,"Or the Sermon on the Plain in St."],[2946.43,"Luke's Gospel, all of these things."],[2948.75,"These are the ways of expressing the fact that you actually believe."],[2954.03,"Where did St"],[2956.85,"Paul get his information about those three years of Jesus' ministry?"],[2963.17,"Well, he, in his own argument, he got it from Christ himself."],[2968.05,"So he couldn't."],[2970.61,"Christ appeared to him not just on the road to Damascus, but multiple times."],[2975.65,"But also, they're not they're not radically different than what's in the Old Testament."],[2983.01,"They are I I disagree."],[2985.81,"I think this is when Christ says, love the Lord your God with all your heart, with all your soul, and with all your mind and with all your strength, and love your neighbor as yourself."],[2995.67,"Which summarizes all those things in the Sermon on the Mount."],[2999.13,"I mean, if you really love your neighbors, those are both quotes."],[3004.17,"From the book of Levitic, from the book of Leviticus."],[3007.85,"He's explaining. No, it's not."],[3009.77,"No, no, no, no"],[3010.13,"He's explaining what that really meant."],[3012.21,"I mean, that he says very nice of the Old Testament understood it."],[3016.61,"That's how the Pharisees understood it."],[3019.74,"The Pharisees understood it, had boiled it down to this checklist."],[3026.43,"Where, you know, well, what does it mean to work on the Sabbath?"],[3029.47,"Well, you can only walk so far."],[3031.63,"And you can only."],[3032.89,"That's not what the Old Testament said."],[3037.79,"They had done that."],[3038.35,"That's why Christ says, unless your righteousness exceeds that of the Pharisees, you will by no means enter the kingdom of heaven."],[3046.77,"Because that's not."],[3048.65,"That's not what it was about."],[3050.09,"He's explaining what it really always meant."],[3055.41,"He's not changing it."],[3057.75,"He's explaining what that always meant."],[3059.59,"Yeah. Turning the other chief in the Old Testament."],[3061.83,"Yes, showing mercy."],[3063.59,"Yeah. Be merciful as your father in heaven is merciful, is a quote from the Old Testament."],[3071.43,"But it had been obscured."],[3073.72,"And is still obscured."],[3075.24,"And so he was explaining the practical level what those things mean, what they really should have been doing."],[3082.44,"Because they became set obsessed with, you know, do I eat shellfish or not?"],[3087.16,"Do I have tassels on my garments?"],[3089.58,"This is what St."],[3090.3,"Paul's arguing against."],[3091.42,"They thought that was what made them righteous people."],[3095.8,"And St. Paul said, that that's not what the Torah was ever about."],[3100.86,"That's not what makes you a righteous person."],[3104.35,"That's not what makes you a righteous person."],[3105.87,"You could do all that and still be horrifically sinful and wicked in your heart."],[3112.53,"But God said that in Deuteronomy."],[3115.57,"Deuteronomy, he said he said, it's not important that you circumcise your flesh."],[3119.89,"It's important that you circumcise your heart."],[3123.89,"Yeah. But they didn't understand that."],[3128.69,"Their whole focus was on the flesh part and not on their heart."],[3135.01,"And so that's what Christ was trying to explain in the in the Sermon on the Mount."],[3138.77,"That's why he said, you know, you say, oh, well, don't commit adultery"],[3143.37,"He says, well, if you're married and you're looking at other women with lust in your heart, you've already broken that commandment."],[3150.19,"In your heart."],[3151.07,"It's not just what you do externally, outside, but it's what's in your heart."],[3156.77,"But that's coming from, I mean, that's in Deuteronomy."],[3158.93,"It's that your heart is what is important."],[3163.01,"They just had lost sight of that."],[3167.49,"So it's it's the same God, and he's teaching the same thing."],[3170.29,"They just hadn't followed it."],[3172.93,"And so Christ is coming to really explain what it means."],[3177.99,"So that they would repent, and then part of repentance is changing your life. and how you live"],[3186.19,"But there's all kinds of, you know, in in uh in the Torah, uh, every fifty years, all property was supposed to revert to the original families that owned it."],[3200.03,"You know? Yeah, in Deuteronomy, there's a commandment, there shall be no poor among you."],[3209.55,"So he's if there's a poor person in Israel, you are all sinning."],[3214.19,"That's in Deuteronomy."],[3218.03,"That's if there were rich and poor."],[3220.19,"So the things Christ says, he's getting back to the original meaning."],[3226.52,"And really applying it."],[3227.24,"Paul ultimately does that too"],[3228.8,"And we're going to see St."],[3232.32,"But it's important that we not read this as saying you don't have to do anything."],[3238.16,"Which is how some of our Protestant friends read it."],[3240.88,"It just matters what you believe in your head or believe in your heart."],[3246.0,"It doesn't matter what you do."],[3249.04,"Whereas St. Paul is very clearly saying that the things that you do reflect what is in your head and in your heart."],[3256.32,"You may claim something else is in your heart."],[3260.4,"But what you do shows what's really there."],[3263.28,"Shows what you really value and what you really think is important."],[3266.48,"And so he's he's trying to get it"],[3269.56,"He's trying to get it just that."],[3272.06,"That's what matters is responding to God in faith and repentance, not keeping a list of rules, as you've defined them, that somehow earn your way to being a righteous person, or earn you the promises of God, or earn you God's forgiveness."],[3290.5,"God offers it, and it's how you respond to his action in grace."],[3296.98,"Whether you cooperate with it or whether you eject it."],[3302.02,"Listen next time as Father Stephen DeYoung continues his study of the scriptures on the whole counsel of God."],[3309.38,"Father Stephen's email address is whole counsel at ancientfaith.com."],[3314.66,"That's whole counsel. at ancientfaith.com"]],"o":[{"t":21.28,"lv":2,"h":"Recap of Romans 1-3 and why summarize the Old Testament","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Starting <b>Romans 4</b>; catching up - going back further than usual</li><li class=\"d0\">Will quickly summarize the <b>Old Testament</b> first: Abraham, Adam, OT folks coming</li><li class=\"d1\">need background to follow Paul's points</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul writing to <b>church at Rome</b>, hasn't been there yet</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Emperor Claudius</b> expelled the Jews; community came back</li><li class=\"d1\">worry: <b>two churches</b> - Jewish Christian and Gentile Christian</li><li class=\"d1\">he wants <b>one church</b>, not splintered by ethnic group</li><li class=\"d0\">Ch. 1-2: historical argument - <b>Gentiles and Jews in the same predicament</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Ch. 3: turns to the <b>law, the Torah</b> and its purpose</li></ul>"},{"t":176.54,"lv":3,"h":"Justification, righteousness and the judgment","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Justification</b> and <b>righteousness</b> = same word complex in Greek, translated differently</li><li class=\"d0\">At stake: <b>Christ returns to judge the living and the dead</b></li><li class=\"d1\">righteous on his <b>right hand</b>, vindicated</li><li class=\"d1\">wicked on his <b>left</b>, condemned</li><li class=\"d1\">everyone receives what is due</li><li class=\"d0\">Question: <b>who is on the righteous side?</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Jewish view (rabbinic Judaism, likely still): <b>Jews righteous, Gentiles wicked</b></li><li class=\"d1\">maybe a few Gentile friends of Israel squeak in</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul <b>dismantles each argument</b> one at a time</li></ul>"},{"t":262.76,"lv":3,"h":"Torah and <i>nomos</i>: not just law","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">One argument: <b>they received the Torah</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><i>Torah</i> = <b>teaching</b>, not \"law\"</li><li class=\"d1\">moral teaching, ordering society, <b>God telling them who he is</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Greek <i>nomos</i> covers laws, customs, culture, religious beliefs - a people's whole way</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>St. Jerome</b> chose <i>lex</i> → English \"law\"</li><li class=\"d1\">boiled down to pure legal structure, commandments</li><li class=\"d1\">\"works of law\" = <b>works commanded by the Torah</b></li><li class=\"d0\">He says Torah after reading \"law\" - first five books include <b>Genesis</b>, narratives too</li><li class=\"d0\">Just <b>receiving</b> it won't help (\"five dollars gets a coffee at Starbucks\")</li><li class=\"d1\">if you don't believe, accept, practice it → it only <b>condemns you</b></li></ul>"},{"t":397.79,"lv":2,"h":"Old Testament summary: Adam's commandment","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">What was the Torah <b>actually for</b>?</li><li class=\"d0\">Genesis: God creates <b>Adam</b> and woman (not named Eve until after the fall)</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>First commandment</b>, before the tree: be fruitful, multiply, fill the earth, subdue it</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Eden</b> = God's garden, place to dwell with humans</li><li class=\"d1\">humans to have kids and <b>spread Eden</b> through the whole world</li><li class=\"d1\">world full of <b>God's presence</b>, with people</li><li class=\"d0\">They did the opposite - <b>Romans 5</b>: spread <b>sin and death</b> instead</li><li class=\"d0\">Christ comes to <b>succeed where Adam failed</b> - turn the cosmos into God's dwelling</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Revelation</b>: no temple - God is everywhere</li><li class=\"d1\">Isaiah: knowledge of God covers the earth <b>as waters cover the sea</b></li></ul>"},{"t":540.89,"lv":2,"h":"Flood and Babel; nations given to angels","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Before Christ: earth corrupted by sin, <b>death and violence</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Climax in <b>Genesis 6</b> - flood washes clean, start over</li><li class=\"d0\">Same commandment to <b>Noah</b> - and same failure: <b>Tower of Babel</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"let's all stay here and build a big city\" - ziggurat temple</li><li class=\"d1\">aim: <b>pull God down</b> to do what we want, not do what he commanded</li><li class=\"d0\">God <b>scatters</b> them by force</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Deuteronomy 32</b>: nations assigned to <b>angelic beings</b>, not to God</li><li class=\"d1\">most fell and became wicked = <b>powers and principalities</b> in Paul</li><li class=\"d1\">gods of the nations = <b>demons</b></li></ul>"},{"t":640.85,"lv":2,"h":"Abraham, Israel and the Torah as a management system","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">God creates a people: grabs <b>Abraham out of Ur</b>, makes promises</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Exodus</b>, creation of Israel - God wants to dwell with them</li><li class=\"d0\">Problem: Israel <b>just as sinful</b> as everyone else (Paul's argument)</li><li class=\"d1\">how can God live among them <b>without destroying them</b> by his holiness?</li><li class=\"d0\">The <b>Torah</b> = system for <b>managing sin and corruption</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>tabernacle</b>, later temple - kept holy for God's dwelling</li><li class=\"d1\">yearly <b>Day of Atonement</b>: two goats</li><li class=\"d1\">one carries sins <b>outside the camp</b>; other sacrificed, blood cleanses stain of the year's sins</li><li class=\"d0\">Must repeat every year - only <b>managing the problem</b></li><li class=\"d1\">alternatives: God leaves or wipes everybody out</li></ul>"},{"t":753.92,"lv":2,"h":"The Torah never made anyone righteous","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Never there to make people righteous</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Keep it perfectly - Paul later says he did: <b>blameless</b> as a Pharisee</li><li class=\"d1\">\"I kept all the rules\" - no credit, just the <b>minimum to not be wicked</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Managing until <b>Christ came</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Major theme of <b>Hebrews</b> and all Paul's epistles</li><li class=\"d1\">old sacrifices annual; high priest first offers for <b>his own sins</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Christ offered himself, <b>perfect sacrifice</b> → management system no longer needed, problem solved</li></ul>"},{"t":831.65,"lv":2,"h":"Paul's argument so far: Gentiles, Jews, exile","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Ch. 1</b>: Gentiles after Babel went astray</li><li class=\"d1\">descended from Noah - once had knowledge of God</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>suppressed</b> it; gave praise and glory to other things → thoroughly corrupt</li><li class=\"d0\">Then the Jews: \"God gave you this - look what you did\"</li><li class=\"d1\">didn't even follow it → land corrupt</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Ezekiel</b>: God gets up and leaves the temple; <b>Babylon</b> wipes it out, exile</li><li class=\"d0\">The Torah can't make you righteous or able to stand at God's right hand</li><li class=\"d1\">opposite: knowledge makes you <b>more subject to judgment</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Gentiles without it have done better than you with it</li></ul>"},{"t":951.01,"lv":2,"h":"End of chapter 3: circumcision and one God","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Focus narrowed to <b>circumcision</b> - one part of the Torah</li><li class=\"d1\">physical emblem of <b>separation</b> from the nations, special status</li><li class=\"d1\">\"we've got special status, we'll be fine; God hates all those other people\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul: God is God of the <b>whole cosmos</b> → God of Gentiles too → judges everyone</li><li class=\"d0\">One God who justifies the circumcised <b>by faith</b>, the uncircumcised <b>through faith</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Faith</b> = same word as the verb <b>believe</b> - not a true/false test</li><li class=\"d1\">= <b>faithful, loyal, true</b> to the knowledge God gave</li><li class=\"d0\">Faithful Gentiles and faithful Jews both on the right hand</li><li class=\"d1\">dividing line not circumcision, eating gator, <b>New Moon festival</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1106.03,"lv":3,"h":"Faithfulness vs keeping the Torah: church attendance","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Faithfulness <b>expressed through</b> keeping Torah - not <b>the same thing</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Modern parallel: we express faith by <b>coming to church on Sunday</b></li><li class=\"d1\">before the judgment seat: \"I went every Sunday\" - <b>won't cut it</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Same with Torah: for Israel, keeping it was part of faithfulness</li><li class=\"d1\">just <b>going through the motions</b>, tassels on your garment</li><li class=\"d1\">\"Well, I never ate shellfish\" - <b>not gonna do it</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1175.69,"lv":2,"h":"Romans 4:1-3 - Abraham our father according to the flesh","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>4:1</b> - \"What then shall we say that Abraham our father has found according to the flesh?\"</li><li class=\"d1\">NKJV ordering odd: <b>Abraham, our father according to the flesh</b>, has found</li><li class=\"d1\">\"we, our\" - still speaking to <b>Jewish people</b>; Abraham their <b>linear descent</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Language matters: <b>John's Gospel</b> - who is really child of Abraham?</li><li class=\"d1\">one who is <b>like Abraham</b> and does what he does, or just descended?</li><li class=\"d1\">Paul makes a similar point coming up</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>4:2</b> - if Abraham was justified by works, he has something to <b>boast about</b>, but not before God</li><li class=\"d1\">if he became a saint by what he did - <b>earned it</b>, could brag</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>4:3</b> - \"Abraham believed God and it was accounted to him for righteousness\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>4:4</b> - wages are <b>debt, not grace</b>; \"the check isn't a gift\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1360.29,"lv":3,"h":"The Genesis context of the quote","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Old Testament quotes: <b>no chapters and verses</b> for another ~1440 years</li><li class=\"d1\">quoting a verse = the <b>whole section</b>, not proof-texting</li><li class=\"d0\">Context: God comes to Abraham in <b>Ur</b> (now Iraq)</li><li class=\"d1\">promises: father of <b>whole nations</b>, great people, <b>land to your seed</b> (Palestine)</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Grace</b> = God acting first</li><li class=\"d1\">no \"40 years of faithful service\" first - God just chooses him</li><li class=\"d0\">Then <b>Abraham's response</b>: he <b>believes God</b></li><li class=\"d1\">in his 70s when he leaves Ur; has a son at <b>100</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"whole nations descended from you\" - and he believes</li></ul>"},{"t":1504.63,"lv":2,"h":"Protestant reading vs Abraham's actions","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Protestant reading: if you work, it's wages; Abraham <b>did nothing</b></li><li class=\"d1\">true: he did nothing <b>to earn the promise</b></li><li class=\"d0\">But he <b>got up</b>, took his whole family <b>hundreds of miles</b> across the Fertile Crescent</li><li class=\"d1\">can't separate his belief from his actions</li><li class=\"d1\">if he'd said \"I believe you\" and gone back to work in Ur - <b>not the same</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Belief includes <b>faithfulness</b> - Paul isn't saying he did nothing</li><li class=\"d0\">Because he responded in belief and action, God counted him <b>righteous</b></li><li class=\"d1\">how he becomes a <b>saint</b>, one of the holy ones - by his <b>response to God's promise</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Protestant version: could sit and \"watch it happen\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1658.08,"lv":3,"h":"David and Jeroboam: promises depend on response","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Opposite examples in the OT</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>David</b>: if you keep my commandments, a descendant will rule forever</li><li class=\"d1\">David did - <b>repentance included</b> in keeping Torah (he sinned plenty)</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Messiah</b> born from his line</li><li class=\"d0\">After <b>Solomon</b> kingdom splits: Judah south, Israel north</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Jeroboam son of Nebat</b> gets the same promise for the north</li><li class=\"d1\">sets up <b>idols</b>; his son assassinated</li><li class=\"d1\">new king - God makes the <b>same promise</b> to him</li><li class=\"d0\">Thought experiment: Abraham says \"I've got things good in Ur\" → God <b>goes to someone else</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1809.36,"lv":2,"h":"The Annunciation: God moves first, we respond","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Same dynamic at the <b>Annunciation</b></li><li class=\"d1\">God to the <b>Theotokos</b>: \"you're going to have a child\"</li><li class=\"d1\">her answer: <b>\"Let it be to me according to your will\"</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Paul isn't arguing God does everything - but <b>God comes to us first</b></li><li class=\"d1\">God didn't wait for Israel in Egypt to get it together - <b>delivered first</b>, then Torah</li><li class=\"d1\">Sinai: \"oh sure, we'll do all this\" - then they don't</li><li class=\"d0\">Paradigm: <b>Christ died before anyone here was born</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Then we respond in faith <b>or don't</b> - faith involves <b>doing something</b></li><li class=\"d1\">for us: Christ returns to judge → <b>changes how we live</b>, view sin and repentance</li><li class=\"d1\">faith and response <b>can't be separated</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1979.79,"lv":2,"h":"Pharisees, exile and the Torah as the way out","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Gospels background: temple rebuilt, some back from exile, still <b>under the Romans</b></li><li class=\"d0\">God's presence <b>hadn't returned</b> to the temple</li><li class=\"d1\">tabernacle dedication: presence, <b>fire, light, cloud</b>; same at temple</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Ezekiel</b>: presence leaves before destruction; nothing like that after rebuild</li><li class=\"d1\">so in their minds <b>still in exile</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Pharisee answer: exiled for not keeping Torah → <b>all keep Torah</b> to end it</li><li class=\"d1\">then Messiah kicks out the Romans, God returns to temple</li><li class=\"d1\">rabbi: if all Jews kept Torah <b>one day</b>, Messiah would come</li><li class=\"d0\">Why Pharisees hated <b>tax collectors and sinners</b>: keeping us <b>under the Roman boot</b></li><li class=\"d1\">also motivated <b>Saul</b> persecuting the church - \"these heretics think Messiah has come\"; corrected by Christ</li></ul>"},{"t":2138.66,"lv":3,"h":"Paul's point: it is the same in the church","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Targeting the idea you <b>make Christ return</b> and be among the righteous <b>by earning it</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Did Abraham get there by <b>perfect Torah life</b>? Torah not yet given</li><li class=\"d1\">no: God came, he responded with <b>faithfulness, loyalty, trust</b>, and acted</li><li class=\"d0\">Same in the church: Jew or Gentile hears the <b>gospel</b></li><li class=\"d1\">respond with faith, <b>change your life</b> → one of the righteous</li><li class=\"d1\">\"don't have to do this other stuff\" - not what it was for</li></ul>"},{"t":2238.49,"lv":2,"h":"Romans 4:5-8 - David and the blessed man","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>4:5</b> - to him who does not work but believes on him who <b>justifies the ungodly</b>, faith accounted for righteousness</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>4:6-8</b> - David: <b>\"blessed are those whose lawless deeds are forgiven</b> and whose sins are covered\"; man to whom the Lord shall not impute sin</li><li class=\"d0\">Q: even after your explanation, I don't follow that paragraph</li><li class=\"d1\">part of the problem: <b>NKJV</b> by Protestants - not nefarious, it's how they read it</li><li class=\"d0\">Protestant reading: Paul quotes David - God just forgives, <b>no work required</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Quote is <b>Psalm 32</b> (31 in Greek numbering)</li></ul>"},{"t":2348.75,"lv":2,"h":"Psalm 32: David confessed","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Reads vv. 1-2 then on: \"because I kept silent my bones grew old\"</li><li class=\"d1\">day and night <b>your hand was heavy</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"I will <b>confess</b> my transgression... and you forgave\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Did this person do nothing? <b>No</b> - David about himself</li><li class=\"d1\">silent → felt wrath, \"like my bones were breaking\"</li><li class=\"d1\">confessed → <b>blessed</b>, sins covered</li><li class=\"d0\">If Paul meant forgiveness without action, <b>lousy passage to quote</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Paul a trained rabbi; readers knew the Psalms - not trying to put one over</li></ul>"},{"t":2464.05,"lv":2,"h":"Forgiveness is grace, yet requires our response","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">\"Work\" here = before the fact: <b>we don't earn forgiveness</b></li><li class=\"d1\">God doesn't say \"your record is so great I'll let this slide\"</li><li class=\"d0\">That God <b>offers forgiveness if we repent</b> = <b>grace</b></li><li class=\"d1\">he could say \"too bad\" - within his rights; <b>Acts</b>: gospel = repentance and forgiveness preached</li><li class=\"d1\">Christ died for our sins <b>before we were born</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Does that mean we do nothing? <b>1 John</b>: \"if we confess our sins, he is faithful and just to forgive\"</li><li class=\"d1\">we must <b>believe it</b> and <b>actually confess, repent, change</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Not mechanical - God doesn't <b>owe</b> forgiveness; but <b>always keeps his promises</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Aside: someone (maybe Charles II) said \"God will forgive me, it's his job\" - <b>not the case</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2642.13,"lv":3,"h":"Forgiveness as healing: Zacchaeus and the unmerciful servant","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Forgiveness = God <b>healing, cleansing, purifying</b> us, not \"I won't smack you later\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Zacchaeus</b> (read Sunday): not just \"sorry for the extortion\"</li><li class=\"d1\">gives <b>half</b> to the poor, repays <b>four times</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Jesus: \"<b>Today salvation has come</b> to this house\" - that's repentance</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Unmerciful servant</b>: forgiven millions, grabs a man owing <b>five bucks</b></li><li class=\"d1\">point isn't \"God lets you slide if you meet requirements\"</li><li class=\"d1\">he <b>hadn't repented</b>, didn't grasp the depth of forgiveness</li><li class=\"d1\">would be <b>forgiving everybody</b>, tearing up creditors</li></ul>"},{"t":2767.36,"lv":3,"h":"\"Just as David also describes\"","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Paul: \"<b>just as David</b> also describes\" - can't argue he says something different</li><li class=\"d0\">God doesn't owe forgiveness but <b>promised</b> it; not earned</li><li class=\"d0\">Receiving it needs <b>belief, faith</b> expressed in <b>actual actions</b></li><li class=\"d1\">forgiveness → <b>repentance</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Abraham → <b>pack up family, travel hundreds of miles</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Q: what were the <b>actions</b> at Paul's time - following Jesus' harder instructions?</li><li class=\"d1\">Zacchaeus: fourfold repayment <b>from the Torah</b> (voluntary); half to poor <b>beyond</b> Torah</li><li class=\"d1\">not a checklist - a <b>whole transformation of your life</b></li><li class=\"d0\">End of Romans: epistles in <b>two parts</b> - theology, then \"therefore\" what to do</li><li class=\"d1\">not tassels and diet: <b>love neighbor</b>, give freely, <b>repent</b>, <b>self-control</b> - not check-offs</li></ul>"},{"t":2933.25,"lv":2,"h":"Q&amp;A: Sermon on the Mount and where Paul got his teaching","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Q: do these add up to Christ's <b>Sermon on the Mount</b>? - yes; also Sermon on the Plain in <b>Luke</b></li><li class=\"d1\">ways of expressing that you <b>actually believe</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Q: where did Paul get his info on the three years of ministry?</li><li class=\"d1\">from <b>Christ himself</b> - appeared multiple times, not just Damascus road</li><li class=\"d0\">Not radically different from the <b>Old Testament</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Christ: love the Lord <b>with all your heart</b>... and neighbor as yourself - both from <b>Leviticus</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>explaining what it really meant</b>, not changing it</li></ul>"},{"t":3010.13,"lv":3,"h":"Pharisees' checklist vs the real Torah","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Pharisees boiled it down to a <b>checklist</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Sabbath: how far can you walk?</li><li class=\"d1\">not what the OT said</li><li class=\"d0\">Christ: unless your righteousness <b>exceeds the Pharisees'</b>, you won't enter the kingdom</li><li class=\"d0\">\"Be merciful as your Father in heaven is merciful\" - from the <b>Old Testament</b>, obscured</li><li class=\"d0\">Obsessed with <b>shellfish and tassels</b> - thought that made them righteous</li><li class=\"d1\">Paul: not what Torah was ever about; could do all that and be <b>wicked in your heart</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Deuteronomy</b>: circumcise your <b>heart</b>, not just flesh</li></ul>"},{"t":3138.77,"lv":2,"h":"Heart, jubilee and original meaning","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Sermon on the Mount: adultery - <b>lust in your heart</b> already breaks it</li><li class=\"d1\">that's in <b>Deuteronomy</b> too - they lost sight of the heart</li><li class=\"d0\">Same God, same teaching; Christ explains it so they <b>repent</b> and change how they live</li><li class=\"d0\">Torah: every <b>fifty years</b> property reverts to original families</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Deuteronomy</b>: \"there shall be <b>no poor among you</b>\" - a poor Israelite means everyone is sinning</li><li class=\"d1\">Christ's sayings = <b>getting back to original meaning</b></li></ul>"},{"t":3227.24,"lv":2,"h":"Summary: respond to God's grace in faith and repentance","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Paul ultimately does the same - applies it</li><li class=\"d0\">Don't read it as \"you <b>don't have to do anything</b>\" - Protestant reading: only what you believe in head or heart</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul: what you do <b>reflects what is in your head and heart</b></li><li class=\"d1\">you may claim otherwise - actions show <b>what you really value</b></li><li class=\"d0\">What matters: <b>responding to God in faith and repentance</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not keeping a list of rules to <b>earn</b> righteousness, promises or forgiveness</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>God offers it; how you respond to his action in grace</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>cooperate with it or reject it</b></li></ul>"}]});
